  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nAn area of high pressure currently rests over the state with a\nsupporting upper level ridge extending through central Montana.\nSouthwesterly flow aloft will provide adequate moisture and QPF`s\nfor widespread snow across the Southwest and South Central areas\nof the state with the previous arrival of colder temperatures\nalready settled in. The mid level flow will be conducive for some\nareas of upslope precipitation enhancement especially for the\nThis ridge will exit the region by Thursday night giving way to a\ndeep Pacific trough propagating into the Mountain West from the\nWest Coast.\n\nAdditionally, a cold core upper low will descend southward from\nAlberta into Northeast Montana by late Saturday morning which will\nbring robust Northwesterly flow across the Western and Central\nportions of the state. This will further cool temperatures down\ninto the mid 20s to low 30s for daily highs and single digit to\nlow teens for lows for the Hi-Line this weekend and early next\nweek.\n\nFor next week, a warming trend will begin by Wednesday as a upper\nridge builds over the region and brings dry conditions back. - RA\n\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nAdditional Snowfall through this Weekend . . . \n\nWith the interaction between a Pacific system to the west and a\ncold core upper low to the north, there is still uncertainty with\nsnowfall amounts across the CWA for this weekend. REFS guidance\nshows widespread convective type snow shower bands that could make\nfor large variations in snowfall distributions for Central and\nNorth Central MT. Highest confidence areas for this region (>\n70%) for snowfall amounts over 1\" will be for the Rocky Mountain\nFront, continental divide, and the Belts. For Southwest MT,\nsnowfall amounts are likely to reach 1 to 4\" for the West\nYellowstone area and up to 3 to 6\" for the Gallatin and Madison Ranges,\nwith a 10% chance for Bozeman proper seeing 6\" or more Friday morning\nthrough the weekend. - RA/Moldan\n\nThe table below lists the snowfall amounts for the \u201clow end\u201d (10th\nPercentile), \u201cmost likely\u201d (50th Percentile), and \u201chigh end\u201d (90th\nPercentile) scenarios within the NBM5.2 for select locations\nacross North Central, Central and Southwest Montana from Friday\nmorning through Sunday morning.\n\nThe low end scenario has a 90% chance that amounts will exceed the\nlisted value or a 10% chance that amounts will be lower than what\nis listed while the high end scenario has a 90% chance that\namounts will be lower than what is listed or a 10% chance that\namounts will be higher than what is listed. The greater the spread\nbetween the low end and the high end scenario the greater the\nuncertainty is with regards to snowfall amounts, with a lower\nspread between low end and high end supporting a lower degree of\nuncertainty (higher confidence).\n\n                   Snow Scenarios Friday AM thru Sunday AM\nLOCATION                 10 Pct  |   50 Pct  |   90 Pct\nBrowning                  0.3\u201d   |    1.9\u201d   |    2.6\u201d\nCut Bank                  0.0\u201d   |    0.9\u201d   |    1.8\u201d\nHavre                     0.0\u201d   |    0.1\u201d   |    0.6\u201d\nGreat Falls               0.4\u201d   |    1.0\u201d   |    2.1\u201d\nLewistown                 0.9\u201d   |    1.5\u201d   |    3.4\u201d\nHelena                    0.0\u201d   |    0.6\u201d   |    2.1\u201d\nBozeman                   0.2\u201d   |    0.9\u201d   |    6.0\u201d\nDillon                    0.0\u201d   |    0.0\u201d   |    2.2\u201d\nEnnis                     0.2\u201d   |    1.5\u201d   |    7.0\u201d\nWest Yellowstone          0.2\u201d   |    1.7\u201d   |    5.8\u201d\n\n- Moldan",
